.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBOperand\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::Operand\fP< dim >::operator= (double val)"
Assignments of CSP basic variables to Operands are implemented as assignment operators in which the type of the variable which is associated with the \fBOperand\fP determines the outcome of the assignment\&. This restricts the number of meaningful assignments for CSP basic variables: Scalars can be assigned to vectors and tensors but not the other way around\&. Vectors cannot be assigned to tensors and vice versa\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIval\fP Constant references to double, \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, and \fBTensorVariable\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The assignments are implemented using the overloaded operators of the CSP basic variables\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Erratic assignments may halt the program and lead to an error report including the name of the \fBOperand\fP to which the assignment was attempted\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> bool \fBcsmp::Operand\fP< dim >::operator> (double val) const"
Operands can be compared with other Operands, double values, and CSP basic variables\&. Whether this comparison is meaningful, depends on the variable types on the left and right-side of the comparitors, and errors will be reported for obviously nonsensical comparisons\&. Comparisons of variables of the same type are straightforward and the comparison of a \fBScalarVariable\fP with a double is as well\&.

.PP
If a vector variable is compared with a scalar, this implementation of comparitors will compare the length of the vector with the scalar, since in most cases this is a physically sensible thing to do\&. 
.br

.PP
If you compare a tensor variable with a scalar, this comparison will use the largest or the smallest element in the tensor, but the comparitors '==' and '!=' are not defined\&. Vectors can 
.br
 not be compared with tensors\&. 
.br

.PP
Only Operands with the same type of associated variables can be compared\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIval\fP A constant reference to any of the types, double, \fBOperand\fP, \fBScalarVariable\fP, \fBVectorVariable\fP, or \fBTensorVariable\fP\&. 
.br
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A boolean variable which evaluates to true or false\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
In as far as possible, the overloaded operators of the basic CSP variables are used to ascertain consistency of CSP variable behaviour\&. No provisions are made for the comparison of enumeration \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP flags\&. This would open a confusing array of possibilities\&. All comparitors are constant methods which means that they do not change the state of Operands\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Typically, you want to compare Operands with floating-point numbers in 'if' statements inside of Calculate() methods of \fBInterrelation\fP subclasses\&. This may involve statements like if ( T > 500\&.0 )\&.\&.\&. This type of comparisons is fully supported by the overloaded comparitors of the \fBOperand\fP class\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The most common runtime message that you may get using a comparitor is

.PP
'cannot compare * variable with * variable'

.PP
To make it easier to track down where this error occurred, the name of the \fBOperand\fP involved in the comparison will be reported as well\&. Remember, however, that temporary Operands have no distinct names\&. 
